  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ebola response 2025-2026 in DRC - Kasai/Bulape</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In September 2025, the Government of the Democratic Republic of Congo officially declared an Ebola Virus Disease (EVD) outbreak affecting the Bulape Health Zone in Kasai Province. The zone hosts approximately 253,686 spread across 19 health areas with 142 villages, including seven affected health areas (Bulape, Bulape Communautaire, Dikolo, Ingongo, Mpianga, Bambalai, and Mpatambamba). An assessment conducted by Oxfam and the Bulape Health Zone Office from  5&#x2013;10 October 2025 identified critical humanitarian needs linked to the prevention and control of infection, access to safe water, hygiene, and sanitation, as well as risk communication and community engagement. At health -facility level, the evaluation found severe Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) and Infection Prevention and Control (IPC) gaps undermining the safety of patients and frontline workers. Four out of seven health centres lack triage or isolation areas, and none have functional IPC committees, sterilization units, or adequate stocks of personal protective equipment (PPE). Even for the three that do, the existing infrastructure is not in good condition.  Health staff are insufficiently trained on IPC standards, and most facilities lack standard operating procedures for cleaning, waste segregation, or safe handling of medical materials. All seven facilities face serious water shortages, relying on distant, unprotected sources, with water purchased at 2,000&#x2013;3,500 Congolese Francs (CDF) per 20 litres. Immediate rehabilitation or construction of seven community water points, storage tanks (2,000 L), and handwashing stations in health facilities is required to ensure safe service delivery. Sanitation conditions are also alarming: most health centres need the construction or rehabilitation of latrines, showers, and seven incinerators and placenta pits  for safe waste disposal.  Oxfam proposes an integrated WASH&#x2013;Infection Prevention and Control (IPC) and Protection response to the Ebola Virus Disease (EVD)</narrative>
  </description>
